  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nBehind this morning's cold front, a reinforcing shot of cool air \nmoves in. That allows today's highs in the 70s, generally south of \nUS-6, to drop back into the 60s everywhere for Monday. When we get \nthe better frost scenarios, it's usually on the heels of a \ntemperature upswing like happens as we head into midweek this week. \nI usually like to see at least 3 hours of 36 to 38F temperatures \nwith light to calm winds and minimal cloud cover to get a good setup \nfor frost. However, it appears that warm advection moves in Monday \nnight and this sets up the chance for mid to upper cloudiness at \nleast in areas west of IN-15 (if you continued it into MI) and north \nof US-6. NAM Bufkit soundings appear to indicate some decoupling, \nbut those mid to upper 30s for a few hours may still be hard to come \nby. The NASA SPoRT LIS actually shows less than 30 percent 0-100 cm \nsoil moisture percentile up in St Joseph to Hillsdale and LaGrange \nto Fulton OH counties, but we've added rain to that area in the last \nweek or so and this may make radiational cooling not entirely as \nefficient as may be needed. This combination of factors would still \npoint to areas north of the Toll road having the best chance for \nfrost, but think more patchy frost as opposed to widespread and this \nmay be relegated to outlying areas. High temperatures stay in the \nupper 60s to upper 70s the rest of the forecast period.\n\nAside from that, dry weather continues. A dry cold front washes out \nand stalls nearby Wednesday into Thursday time period before pushing \nback northeastward Friday into Saturday. The ECMWF has rain in the \nvicinity of our area Thursday night, but its moisture column \nlooks rather dry overhead. The next chance for rain could come \nas early as Saturday night, but may wait until Sunday/Sunday \nnight. This is as a deep cutoff low makes its way northward from\nthe south/southwest bringing moisture with it. It has times of \nlarge scale ascent that may be able to squeeze moisture out, but\nlittle instability.",
